<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Process> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ProcessOperator> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#State> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#SystemLimit> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#TechnicalResource> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The structural connection through which the execution of a process operator is assigned to a technical resource."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Assignment"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Assignment> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 1, Figure 3 carries this relation as the usage symbol, an edge of the graphical notation.\n\nReifying the assignment as a class is a design decision of this pattern, taken for the same reasons as the reification of the flow. The VDI 3682 ODP of Helmut Schmidt University models it as the object property isAssignedTo."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Process> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ProcessOperator> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#State> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#SystemLimit> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#TechnicalResource> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A property of an object of the formalised process description. Two kinds of attribute exist, the identification and the characteristics."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Attribute"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 2, Section 5 and Figure 3."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An attribute describing a process specific property of an object. It consists of a category, a descriptive element and a relational element."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Characteristic"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Characteristic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 2, Section 5.2 and Figures 5 to 7.\n\nThe standard fixes this three-part structure but does not prescribe a vocabulary of properties. The entries shown in its figures, such as setpoint value or validity limits, are examples closed by an open placeholder. Non-functional properties of resources therefore have to draw their vocabulary from a further standard."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#State> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Product> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Energy required or released by a process operator, for example electrical energy or heat. Energy occurs as input and as output of a process."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Energy"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Energy> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 1, Section 4.1."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Process> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ProcessOperator> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#State> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#SystemLimit> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#TechnicalResource> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A directed connection transporting a product, an energy or an information between a state and a process operator."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Flow"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 1, Figure 3 lists the flow as one of the eight symbols of the graphical notation, where it is an edge rather than an object.\n\nReifying the flow as a class is a design decision of this pattern and deviates from the VDI 3682 ODP of Helmut Schmidt University, which models flows as object properties. The reification is required because the AIAS alignment ontology treats flows as instances of Relation so that the modelling assistance can annotate them, and because the graphical notation of this work carries a class field and a naming field inside every symbol, which an edge cannot hold.\n\nThe direct properties hasInput and hasOutput are retained and inferred from the reified flows through property chains, so that reasoning over predecessor and successor is unaffected by the reification."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Attribute> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The attribute uniquely identifying an object and its characteristics over the life cycle of a process description."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Identification"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Identification> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 2, Section 5.1, after IEC 61360-1. It carries a unique ident, a long name, a short name, references to external identification systems, a version number and a revision number."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#State> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Product> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Information required by a process operator to carry out its transformation, or information created by it."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Information"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Information> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 1, Section 4.1. Information leaving a process is the point of contact with the communication pattern of the AIAS information model."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#Flow> .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A flow that is part of a partially shared flow denoting process steps running concurrently."@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Parallel Flow"@en .
<https://w3id.org/aias/odp/vdi3682#ParallelFlow> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "VDI 3682 Part 1, Section 4.2.1 recommends modelling parallel process runs as a partially shared flow and explicitly requires no additional graphical elements for them. Introducing a class for the parallel flow is therefore a design decision of this pattern rather than an adoption from the standard.\n\nA parallel flow states that a single connection belongs to a concurrent run. Grouping several connections into one conjunction is expressed through the dependency property between the states involved."@en .
# 
